  "termination": "completed",
  "answer": "I fixed the parser so chunk boundaries no longer affect results.\n\n**What was wrong**\n- `src/line.mjs` treated a bare `\\r` as a record ending, so `\\r` at a chunk boundary ended a record instead of waiting for the following `\\n` (and a bare CR was never reported as `BARE_CR`).\n- In `src/state.mjs`, the `afterQuote` state rejected a second `\"` (`INVALID_QUOTE`) instead of treating `\"\"` as an escaped quote, so doubled quotes split across chunks failed.\n- `pendingCR` was set but never actually used to defer CR handling.\n\n**Changes**\n- `src/state.mjs`: rewrote `feedChar` so a CR outside quotes sets `pendingCR` and is resolved by the next character (`\\n` ends the record, anything else throws `BARE_CR`); `finishState` still throws `BARE_CR` for a trailing CR. In `afterQuote`, `\"` now appends a literal `\"` and returns to quoted mode, while `,`, CR, LF, or end of input close the field/record and anything else throws `INVALID_QUOTE`. Record ending is centralized in `endRecord`, which finishes the field and record and resets mode/CR state.\n- `src/line.mjs`: emptied (kept as a module) since line handling moved into the state machine; nothing else imported it.\n\n`parseRows`, `readItems`, `countItems`, the consumers, schema validation, and the slug/checksum helpers are untouched, so both consumers still share one parser result and propagate the same errors.\n\n**Checked:** only by reading the code and public tests — no tests were executed (no shell available).",
